<h2>Why Walkways Deserve More Than a Row of Stakes</h2>
<p>Picture a guest arriving at a Westlake Hills home at 8:30 on a December evening. The driveway drops toward the garage, the front walk curves around a live oak, and three limestone steps rise to the porch. Without lighting, every one of those transitions is a guess. With a row of identical stakes every four feet, the walk is lit but looks like an airport taxiway. The path lighting Austin TX homeowners actually want sits between those extremes: enough light to see every edge and change in elevation, placed so quietly that nobody thinks about the fixtures at all.</p>
<p>Path lighting is also the layer that carries the most liability. Trip-and-fall injuries happen on steps and at grade changes, and Hill Country lots have plenty of both. Austin Landscape Lighting treats walkway lighting as a safety system first and a design element second, which is why it is included in nearly every one of the 500+ projects we have completed since 2020.</p>

<h2>Our Approach to Designing Path Lighting</h2>
<h3>Light the surface, hide the source</h3>
<p>Every path fixture we install is fully shielded. The lamp sits under a brass or copper hat so you see a pool of light on the ground, not a glowing bulb. This matters for comfort, for dark-sky courtesy and for your eyes: a single unshielded path light can make the rest of the yard look darker than it is because your pupils adjust to the bright point.</p>
<h3>Spacing by surface and situation</h3>
<p>We do not space fixtures at a fixed interval. Instead we place them where the walk turns, where it narrows, where steps begin and end, and where a planting bed meets the paving. Alternating sides of the path breaks the runway look and lets each pool of light overlap slightly with the next. The table below shows the planning spacings we start from before adjusting for your specific layout.</p>
