<li><strong>Low bollards</strong> for driveways and commercial walks where a taller light shaft is appropriate.</li>
<li><strong>Downlights</strong> mounted in trees or on eaves to light a path from above, often the cleanest solution under a live oak canopy.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Color temperature, lumens and wiring</h3>
<p>Path lights run at 2700K and typically 100 to 200 lumens each, far less than people expect. The goal is a soft, even surface, and overlapping low-output pools beat a few bright ones every time. Fixtures connect to 12-gauge direct-burial cable with waterproof connectors and run back to a stainless transformer with a photocell and astronomical timer. Because path runs are long and spread out, we size the cable and split circuits to keep voltage drop under one volt at the farthest fixture. The <a href="/tools/transformer-calculator/">transformer calculator</a> shows how fixture count and run length interact if you want to understand the sizing.</p>

<h2>Austin Considerations We Design Around</h2>
<p><strong>Terrain and grade changes.</strong> Few Austin lots are flat. Steps, terraced beds and sloped drives are the norm from Bee Cave to Georgetown, and every grade change gets its own fixture rather than relying on light spilling over from the walk.</p>
<p><strong>Live oaks and leaf litter.</strong> Live oaks drop leaves in spring and acorns in fall, and either can bury a low path light. We set fixture height with the canopy in mind and often recommend one or two tree-mounted downlights where a path passes under heavy cover. Our <a href="/services/garden-and-tree-lighting/">garden and tree lighting</a> page covers that technique.</p>
<p><strong>Mowing and string trimmers.</strong> Fixtures placed in turf get hit. We position path lights inside bed edges whenever possible and use heavy brass stems that survive a glancing blow when turf placement is unavoidable.</p>
<p><strong>Deer.</strong> In Lakeway, Rollingwood and Dripping Springs, deer walk the same paths you do. They rarely damage brass fixtures, but they do knock plastic ones over, which is one more reason we do not install them.</p>
<p><strong>Dark-sky and HOA guidance.</strong> Fully shielded path lights at 2700K with modest output are about as dark-sky friendly as outdoor lighting gets. Light goes down, not out or up. HOAs across the region accept this approach, and we provide a layout drawing when a committee requests one.</p>

